course: dictionary
lesson: 6840
title: Outlet: a way out
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An outlet is a way for something to get out. Water can leave a lake through an outlet. In American and Canadian English, an outlet is also a wall socket, where electricity comes out so you can plug things in.
explain: Read the clues around the word. «Checking can show something is real: several big outlets report it.» Several big outlets reporting the same story tells you outlets must be places that share news, such as newspapers, TV channels or websites.
explain: An outlet can also be a way of letting out feelings or energy. Sport or art can be an outlet for anger or stress. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: An outlet is a way for something to get out, such as water leaving a lake.
fact: In American and Canadian English, an outlet is a wall socket where you plug in electrical things.
fact: An outlet can be a shop that sells things, often cheaply, such as a factory outlet.
fact: In the news, «outlets» means places that report news, such as newspapers, TV channels and websites.
fact: Never poke anything into an outlet (socket), and keep water away from it.
exercise: «Several big outlets reported the story.» What does outlets mean here? | Teachers who share their lessons | *Places that report news, such as newspapers and TV channels | Holes where water leaks out | Pieces of gold jewellery
exercise: Lucy says painting is her outlet for stress. What does outlet mean here? | A shop that sells paintings | A broken pipe | *A way of letting out feelings | A wall socket for her paints
exercise: A factory outlet is a shop. What might a factory outlet sell? | Only electrical plugs and sockets | Only news reports | Only water from a lake | *Things, often cheaply
test: In American English, what is an outlet? | *A wall socket where you plug in electrical things | A news report that is always true | A place where water leaves a lake | A way of letting out energy only
test: Why does the author say «no other outlets» is a warning clue? | The headline is too short | The story is very popular with readers | The photo is very old | *Real big news is usually reported by many outlets
exam: Which sentence uses outlet to mean a way of letting out energy? | She plugged the lamp into the outlet. | *Running around the park is a good outlet for his energy. | The shop's outlet is closed on Sundays. | The river's outlet is at the bottom of the hill.
